#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106917} :activity/id #uuid "75a4e4a8-271a-52f6-80d3-a6bbb0833cfd" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050149 :topic/id #uuid "30edc058-70f2-5a72-9472-b7c90e677423" :topic/math-academy-id 1275} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106917 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107063 :tutorial/content "The formula for the area of a general triangle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=\\frac{1}{2}{\\color{red}{a}}{\\color{blue}{b}}\\sin{C},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\color{red}a$ and $\\color{blue}b$ are two sides of the triangle and $C$ is the measure of the angle between those sides (called the **included angle**).\n\nWe'll prove this result at the end of the lesson.\n\nTo demonstrate how this works, suppose that we want to calculate the area of the non-right-angled triangle below:\n\n![](images/4b/4b0fb75f9b2eaf3d0237d8cfe2e31bc27b02425b6cffa8d15f27d6de03f60838.png)\n\nLet's call the lengths of the sides $BC = \\color{red}{a}$ and $AC ={\\color{blue}{b}}.$ For the triangle above we have\n${\\color{red}a=10},$ ${\\color{blue}b=12},$ and  $m\\angle C=45^{\\circ}.$\n\nTherefore, the area of our triangle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 10\\cdot 12\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{45}^{∘} \\\\  & =60\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =30\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ff9f9791-f688-59c5-8c9d-b776016045be" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2465 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e26490ba-2418-5a78-9c31-5425d1b67c42" :step/math-academy-id 463833 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106918}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106918 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105903 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105560 :question/id #uuid "62405619-31ca-5403-aa45-360e6b3fbd59" :question/math-academy-id "e-3289"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "acc86fb6-2ef4-5f8f-b204-6e1fdbbf0530" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049449}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528224} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106086} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295109} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295110} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295123} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528157} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528162} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528169} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528172} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528204} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528213} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528214}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area of a Triangle"} :step/id #uuid "ee7e485d-cba2-595c-b5bd-d8cd98cc7e04" :step/math-academy-id 463834 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106919}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106919 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105904 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105579 :question/id #uuid "c8d31ea4-41c6-5f24-bf7a-a950a30342d7" :question/math-academy-id "e-4253"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cafdb077-0e02-5e7a-bf78-c3a8aacfb6f7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050847}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106347} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295125} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295126} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323821} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329721}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Angle or Side of a Triangle Given the Area of a Triangle"} :step/id #uuid "ca664e6a-cebd-5079-927f-7fc062b9c6fd" :step/math-academy-id 463837 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106920}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106920 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105905 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105561 :question/id #uuid "b41b2354-c339-5398-ab43-5886b8447c1c" :question/math-academy-id "e-3294"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3173d361-b91d-5699-97d8-6595ef59d0a3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106087} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106439} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295111} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297160} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297161} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335939} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335940} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341859}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area of a Quadrilateral"} :step/id #uuid "ab2a0c05-ebeb-54b0-a9a3-b37d1f143e15" :step/math-academy-id 463840 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106921}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106921 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105906 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105562 :question/id #uuid "472216c8-da7d-58fb-9056-e71d5e33460d" :question/math-academy-id "e-3297"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dd3dcff-06d2-5d61-9863-0f5b541d8d4b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106088} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106089} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297162} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297163} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297164} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297165} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341858}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area of a Triangle Given the Area of a Quadrilateral"} :step/id #uuid "85b3474e-aa3e-5a9b-8c47-069d6e314fad" :step/math-academy-id 463843 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106922}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106922 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107027 :tutorial/content "Consider $\\triangle ABC$ with sides of lengths $a,b,$ and $c,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/1d/1d30e12e2252b27a651bb02b718cf2214c7ddcf4f0e768c5bd952c11b2e4ecde.png)\n\nLet's now add the height of length $h$ perpendicular to $\\overline{BC},$ as shown.\n\n![](images/96/96817ab2168b9ec37777166a26001ee8bc7e7709f80fdf234ac6d70cdfbb5ddc.png)\n\nUsing the usual formula for the area $\\mathcal A$ of a triangle in terms of its base and height, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A = \\dfrac12 ah.\n$$\n\nUsing elementary trigonometry, we have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{h}{b} = \\sin C\n$$\n\nwhich means that \n\n$$\nh = b\\sin C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by substituting this into our expression for $\\mathcal A,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A = \\dfrac12ab\\sin C\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nWe can use a similar approach to prove the following formulas:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}A \\\\ A & =\\frac{1}{2}ac\\operatorname{sin}B\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e126d83f-0c01-514b-bb7e-ce2d16291b22" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13277 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Formula for the Area of a General Triangle"} :step/id #uuid "29c5a503-b01d-5845-a29d-0bd76807b33f" :step/math-academy-id 463846}] :activity/title "The Area of a General Triangle" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105954}]}
